<!DOCTYPE airframe SYSTEM "../airframe.dtd">
<airframe name="A160">
  <description>
    Small glider type fixedwing airframe with Lisa MXS flightcontroller hardware.

    + Model:       WLToys XK A800 glider
    + FC:          Lisa MXS v1.0 with a MPU6000, not a MPU9250
    + Actuators:   Default aileron servos but Rudder and Elevator replaced by 2.3g servos
    + GNSS         Ublox M10 GNSS set to 19Hz output rate with 4 GNSS constellations 
    + MAG          External IST8310 Magnetometer on GNSS device
    + ESC:         HAKRC AM32 35A
    + BEC:         2.5A small BEC
    + RCRX:        OpenRXSR Receiver
    + AIRSPEED:    Sensirion SDP33 differential pressure sensor via I2C
    + TELEMETRY:   Si10xx Chip based with firmware enabling PPRZ RSSI message
    + CURRENT:     No current sensor
    + RANGER:      None
    + MOTOR:       NoName Brushless motor, 1306 18mm dia, 2750Kv with 2mm shaft
    + PROP:        Default of set

    NOTES:

    + Flightcontroller PCB is rotated on Z axis 45 degrees counterclockwise and flipped 180 degrees
    + Telemetry powered via BEC on flightcontrollen (100mAh max + MCU 60mAh is less than 250mAh, the max the DC converter can handle )
    + Uploading the firmware is performed with Black Magic Probe (BMP) adapter
    + External magnetometer resides on same PCB as GNSS
    + Uses PAPARAZZI "standard" radio channel settings
    + Frequency units are in Hz
    + OpenRXSR Receiver set to CPPM out and Failsafe to NOPULSE
    + On the IST8310 magneto on GNSS there will be a random 4 digit batch number e.g. 1430, a newline then 010

    BAT voltage:
    With our Lisa MXS the resistor devider is 10k and 17k to GND. 
    This goes to a max of 6.6v measururing, not enough for 2S we need a range to 8.4v
    So via e.g.  https://ohmslawcalculator.com/voltage-divider-calculator
    But we want 8.4v max that is 10k on one side and 6k6 on the other
    Looking at Lisa MXS schematics then R10 needs to be replaced by a 6k6 resistor
    Or place a resistor on top, easier soldering. We only had a 7k0 lying around at the time, 
    so our multiplication factor is now ~0.0023872f you find it int the BAT section


  </description>

